module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.an_unreached_entry_body_is_never_typechecked

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data an_unreached_entry_body_is_never_typechecked: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "an_unreached_entry_body_is_never_typechecked" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**an unreached entry body is never typechecked, so an unresolved symbol inside it is green everywhere** (INVALID STATE: a declaration body -- characteristically an effectful `func` entry, invoked by an operator or a workflow rather than by any witness -- references an identifier that is neither imported nor locally declared. Resolution is CLOSURE-SCOPED: a claim run resolves the items reachable from its entry, so a body nothing reaches is never handed to the typechecker, and the defect is indistinguishable from correct code to every automated reader. ",

    "HARM, AND ITS SHAPE IS THE POINT: THE FAILURE IS DEFERRED ONTO THE OPERATOR AT THE MOMENT OF USE. Nothing is wrong until somebody actually runs the entry -- which is precisely when a human is standing at a terminal expecting an effect, usually the sort of effect that takes a host for an hour or pulls ten gigabytes. The cost lands on the person least able to absorb it and furthest from the edit, which is DESIGN section 2's deferred cost in its sharpest form. And because the symptom is a resolution error rather than a wrong answer, it reads as the tool being broken rather than as a change having been made. ",

    "DISTINGUISHING FACT, AND IT IS WHAT MAKES THIS A LEDGER ROW RATHER THAN A TYPO: EVERY AUTOMATED WALL PASSES. Measured on gunbc#11107, head 59848860ec992ac8d9d5551508eab6e50ad816bc, where `gunbc.spark.vllm_runtime_image_build` imports three names from `gunbc.spark.host_commitment` at line 13 and uses a fourth, `spark_uncommitted_hosts`, at line 937 inside `spark_build_host_reachability_wet`. All four required lanes reported success on that head -- heal-generated-artifacts, required-witnesses-build, required-witnesses-floor and the witnesses aggregate -- and five local witness suites covering the changed modules reported 14/14, 22/22, 18/18, 2/2 and 21/21 with no error lines. The re-derivation is `gh run view 34666870734 --json jobs` against that commit, beside `git show 59848860:dag/gunbc/spark/vllm_runtime_image_build.dag`. ",

    "**HOW IT IS ACTUALLY WRITTEN, because it is not carelessness and the mechanism matters.** Both specimens came from TRIMMING AN IMPORT LIST, not from adding a call. A review found an unused type name in an import, the author removed it, and removed a sibling name that WAS used -- by a body the author's tests do not reach and therefore never saw. The second specimen is the same edit in the same session: `spark_host_standing` in `test.claim.spark.spark_host_commitment_witness`. So the population to watch is not new code but SHRINKING IMPORT LISTS, which is exactly the edit a section 3c dangling-declaration finding provokes. Removing an unused name and removing a used one are the same keystroke, and only one of them has a wall. ",

    "WHAT SEPARATES THIS FROM ITS NEIGHBOURS. `specification-without-execution` (DESIGN section 5) is about a consumer that typechecks but never runs; here the body does not even typecheck, and the reason nobody notices is the same closure boundary. `a_written_row_is_not_a_firing_mechanism` is about an enrolled-looking artifact that no mechanism reads; this is about a body every mechanism DECLINES to read because nothing demands its value. The shared root is that reachability is the unit of work in this compiler, and the shared remedy is not shared: that one is fixed by enrolling the row, this one by typechecking outside any closure. ",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: MITIGATABLE, and barely -- the only wall that fired was a human reading the diff. Two independent reviewers found the first specimen; the author found the second only by running a static import check after being told about the first. No test, no lane, and no local run distinguishes the defective head from a correct one. **CEILING: 4, structurally impossible, and the reason it is decidable is that both operands are in the Node tree**: a body's free identifiers and its module's import list and local declarations are all ordinary declarations, so 'every identifier in every body resolves' is a total question over the accepted corpus, asked without executing anything. Nothing about it is undecidable and nothing waits on a modelling authority; it waits only on someone asking it. ",

    "**NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, NAMED AS THE CAPABILITY: a pass that resolves the identifiers of EVERY declaration body in the compiled corpus, including bodies outside every witness closure and every entry closure, and refuses an unresolved one with its module, symbol and position.** SUFFICIENT FOR: a module that uses a name it does not import refuses at acceptance, whether or not any witness, entry or workflow reaches the body that uses it. A trigger naming a lens over `func` declarations specifically would be satisfied while the same hole stayed open for any other unreached body, and a trigger naming 'add a witness for the reachability entry' would be satisfied by one witness while the class stayed alive across the corpus -- which is DESIGN section 4b(3)'s grain mismatch, a corpus loss answered by a per-site trigger. ",

    "RECOGNITION RULE, ONE QUESTION, ASKED WHEN AN IMPORT LIST GETS SHORTER: which bodies use the name being removed, and is any of them a body my tests do not reach? The behavioural tell is an edit that deletes an import entry in response to an unused-declaration finding, in a module that carries `func` entries or other operator-invoked bodies. The cheap local check until the wall exists: collect the import and local-declaration names of the changed module, then grep its body text for each exported name of the authorities it imports from -- which is the check that found the second specimen here.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
